    private var hybridVTOLRouteNodeDiagnostic: String?
    /// The altitude the navigation obstacle band is built around for a hybrid VTOL: the active
    /// leg's route altitude, and deliberately *not* whatever this tick's guidance branch commanded.
    ///
    /// `fixedWingAutopilotAltitudeCommand` is rewritten every tick with the chosen branch's target,
    /// which for a VTOL is the route altitude when the route is available and the latched hold
    /// altitude when it is not. Feeding that into the band closed a loop through the obstacle cache:
    /// branch → altitude command → band → obstacle set → route validity → branch. Measured in
    /// flight, the band alternated between 20-89 m and 78-94 m about once a second, the obstacle
    /// set with it (190 ↔ 18 784), and every flip re-filtered the world and re-rasterised the A*
    /// grid — guidance went from 0.13 ms to 3.5-6 ms a tick and the simulation from 60 to 20 Hz,
    /// while the aircraft alternately descended toward the route altitude and climbed back to the
    /// hold, porpoising 92 → 65 → 92 m instead of flying the leg.
    private var hybridVTOLNavigationBandAltitude: Float?
    /// Whether the tailsitter's one-time departure rotation has been completed on this route.
    ///
    /// The align-before-transition gate is a *departure* gate, and it is entered on transition
    /// progress below 0.08 — a condition the aircraft re-satisfies every time the transition dips,
    /// hundreds of metres later and hundreds of metres up. Re-entering it there commanded the
    /// departure altitude again: measured mid-mission at 90 m with the route at 89 m, the gate
    /// asked for 20 m on alternate ticks, so the aircraft rocked between starting the transition
    /// and dropping back to hover, and flew the first two legs as a half-copter. Alignment onto a
    /// later leg belongs to the wing controller; this gate runs once.
    private var hybridVTOLDepartureAlignmentComplete = false
    /// Last transition-lever value actually issued, and when its push/no-push class last changed.
    /// When the transition first became "low and still sinking", or nil while it is not.
    private var hybridVTOLAltitudeAbortSince: Float?
    /// How long that has to hold before it counts as the transition failing rather than sagging.
    private static let hybridVTOLAltitudeAbortDwellSeconds: Float = 1.0
    /// Past this transition progress the altitude abort is no longer an economy: giving the wing
    /// back costs the same actuator travel as finishing, so there is nothing left to save by
    /// stopping. Real sink is still caught by the sink-rate abort at any progress.
    private static let hybridVTOLAltitudeAbortMaximumProgress: Float = 0.80
    private var hybridVTOLTransitionLeverLatched: Double?
    private var hybridVTOLTransitionLeverLatchedSince: Float?
    /// How long a transition-lever class is held before the opposite one may be issued. Sized to
    /// the tilt actuator, which needs about two seconds to answer an order at all.
    private static let hybridVTOLTransitionLeverDwellSeconds: Float = 1.6
    /// Whether this leg has committed to completing the wing transition.
    ///
    /// Entering the wing is a decision about the leg, not a per-tick opinion about the current
    /// lift ratio. Re-deciding every tick from instantaneous lift and airspeed, against a tilt
    /// actuator that needs ~2 s for full travel, is an oscillator: measured over fifteen
    /// consecutive ticks of one flight, `prog` went 0.29, 0.60, 0.16, 0.41, 0.05, 0.38, 0.25,
    /// 0.11, 0.26, 0.23, 0.34, 0.20, 0.38, 0.05, 0.22 with `why` alternating
    /// `vtol_transition_to_cruise` / `_delayed_lift_or_speed` / `_abort_liftReserveLost` and `vy`
    /// swinging +3.2 to -3.1 m/s. The aircraft never reached the wing and struck a building
    /// mid-oscillation, still rotor-borne.
    ///
    /// Once the aircraft has demonstrated the lift and speed the transition asks for, hold the
    /// lever until something genuinely disqualifies the transition — sink beyond the limit, an
    /// obstacle, or the physics step reporting the tilt blocked. Those are the aborts already
    /// evaluated ahead of the latch, and each of them clears it.
    private var hybridVTOLTransitionCommitted = false
    /// Share of the shorter of the two legs meeting at a corner that the cruise turn may consume
    /// on one side of it. Half is the geometric ceiling — the corner at the leg's far end has an
    /// equal claim on the same straight — so this sits just under half.
    ///
    /// Judgement, not measurement. What is measured is the geometry it is applied to: a 68 m
    /// cruise turn radius needs 68 m of run-out for a right-angle corner, on legs a city mission
    /// makes about 140 m long.
    private static let hybridVTOLCornerCruiseLegFraction: Float = 0.45
    /// Below this the polyline is straight enough that no turn is being flown at all (~6 deg).
    private static let hybridVTOLNegligibleCornerRadians: Float = 0.10
    /// Half-angle cap that keeps `tan` finite at a course reversal (~83 deg).
    private static let hybridVTOLMaximumCornerHalfAngleRadians: Float = 1.45
    /// Seconds a tilt-rotor takes to come out of the wing and settle into hover, end to end.
    ///
    /// This used to be sized from the tilt actuator alone — "the servo needs about two seconds for
    /// full travel" — with a separate rotor-borne braking term added on top. Both halves described
    /// pieces of the manoeuvre rather than the manoeuvre, and together they came to about 3.35 s of
    /// travel, against the 6-7 s the aircraft actually takes. Everything sized from it was therefore
    /// short by roughly half: a Wingcopter gave the wing up 105 m out at 25 m/s and was still
    /// wing-borne at `wb=0.68` when it met a building, and a Quantum hit one at 21.7 m/s with the
    /// transition at `prog=0.31` and still falling.
    ///
    /// So it is one figure now, covering the whole transition including the speed it sheds, and it
    /// is the operator's measurement rather than a derivation: 7 s, the top of the observed range,
    /// because arriving early costs seconds and arriving late costs the airframe. Operator's call,
    /// 2026-08-20; tune here.
    private static let hybridVTOLTiltRotorTransitionOutSeconds: Float = 7.0
    /// The same figure for a tailsitter, which does not have an actuator in the loop at all.
    ///
    /// Not a measurement — the operator's 6-7 s was explicitly not for this rig, and no replacement
    /// has been flown yet. It is the sum the aircraft was previously sized by (2.0 s of nominal
    /// transition plus 1.35 s/(m/s) of rotor braking) and flew a clean approach on: it took its
    /// first waypoint, went to rotors and stepped back onto the point. Restored rather than derived,
    /// and the first tailsitter measurement should replace it. See
    /// `hybridVTOLTransitionOutSeconds()`.
    private static let hybridVTOLTailsitterTransitionOutSeconds: Float = 3.35
    /// Length of the hover bridge the protected route now being flown ends with, or zero. Carried out
    /// of `safeFixedWingRoute` rather than through the tracking-context types because only the
    /// regime decision consumes it. See `FixedWingSafeRoute.approachBridgeMeters`.
    private var hybridVTOLRouteApproachBridgeMeters: Float = 0.0
    /// Metres added to this leg's route altitude because no route existed at the lower one.
    ///
    /// A mission waypoint carries no altitude — `MissionTarget.position` is planar and the
    /// autopilot supplies `routeAltitude` — so a waypoint "inside a building" is really a planar
    /// column that intersects geometry *at one altitude*. And the planner's obstacle set is
    /// altitude-banded: anything whose roof is below the band is not in its world at all. Climbing
    /// is therefore not a workaround for a blocked route, it is the mechanism the system already
    /// uses, and refusing to fly instead was a planning failure dressed up as a safety property.
    /// A multirotor on the same map never refuses — its marker route goes straight to
    /// `AutoPathPlannerService`, which projects occupied endpoints and flies.
    private var hybridVTOLRouteAltitudeLift: Float = 0.0
    private var hybridVTOLRouteBlockedSeconds: Float = 0.0
    /// Long enough that a single blocked tick from a transient planner timeout does not move the
    /// aircraft, short enough that an operator does not watch it hover while it decides.
    private static let hybridVTOLRouteLiftDelaySeconds: Float = 2.0
    /// One storey at a time. A large step would clear most city geometry in one go but would also
    /// abandon a corridor that was passable ten metres up, which is the altitude the mission asked
    /// for in the first place.
    private static let hybridVTOLRouteLiftStepMeters: Float = 12.0
    /// Above this ratio of planned length to straight-line distance, a plan is a maze rather than a
    /// detour, and the aircraft is better off climbing than threading it.
    ///
    /// Measured over imported Manhattan on one WingtraOne flight: its genuine detours came back at
    /// 1.04x and 1.13x of the direct line and flew cleanly; the street maze that produced the crawl
    /// came back at 2.03x (`pts=61 len=1616 dir=797`). 1.6 separates them with room on both sides.
    private static let hybridVTOLRouteMazeLengthRatio: Float = 1.6
    /// Below this the ratio means nothing: a short leg's plan is dominated by the endpoint
    /// projections, not by the geometry it is avoiding.
    private static let hybridVTOLRouteMazeMinimumDirectMeters: Float = 40.0
    /// Fastest a hybrid VTOL is allowed to translate on its rotors.
    ///
    /// Rotor-borne is not a slower version of wing-borne, it is a different envelope: lean buys
    /// about 2.8 m/s^2 of lateral acceleration, so cross-track error is corrected on a timescale of
    /// `speed / 2.8` seconds. At the 24 m/s a Wingcopter was measured holding in
    /// `vtol_hover_hold_short_leg`, recovering the 12.1 m of clearance its own route had certified
    /// takes longer than the leg lasts, which is why it reached the facade instead of the waypoint.
    /// At 12 m/s it is about a second and a half, and the certified corridor is a corridor again.
    ///
    /// 12 sits below every profile's `minSustainableSpeedMps` (the lowest is 10), so it is
    /// unambiguously rotor territory and can never fight the wing for a leg the wing should have.
    private static let hybridVTOLRotorBorneTranslationCeilingMps: Float = 12.0
    /// Sideways acceleration a rotor-borne aircraft can actually produce.
    ///
    /// The hover law clamps lean at ±16°, and `g·tan(16°)` is 2.81 m/s². Anything that plans a
    /// lateral displacement has to be measured against this or it is planning a move the aircraft
    /// cannot make.
    private static let rotorBorneLateralAccelerationMps2: Float = 2.8
